## Tor configuration for the Tor-Mail onion service (v3 only).
## Reference: https://community.torproject.org/onion-services/setup/
##            https://2019.www.torproject.org/docs/tor-manual.html.en (HiddenService* options)

DataDirectory /var/lib/tor
Log notice stdout
AvoidDiskWrites 1

## This daemon only publishes an onion service. It is not a relay, exit, or SOCKS proxy, and has no control port.
SocksPort 0
ClientOnly 1
ExitRelay 0

## The onion service. Everything under HiddenServiceDir is secret: hs_ed25519_secret_key IS the service identity.
## Losing it means a new onion address; leaking it lets someone impersonate the service. Back it up (encrypted).
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/tormail/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
## Forward to the application's Unix socket (shared volume). Deliberately NOT a TCP address: Tor Browser sends
## "Secure" cookies over plain-HTTP onion connections, so the Tor-to-app hop must stay local and off the network.
## https://onionservices.torproject.org/apps/web/onionspray/security/advisories/001-torbrowser/
HiddenServicePort 80 unix:/run/tormail/app.sock

## Abuse and DoS hardening. Behind Tor the application cannot see client addresses, so limits live here as well.
HiddenServiceMaxStreams 200
HiddenServiceMaxStreamsCloseCircuit 1
HiddenServiceEnableIntroDoSDefense 1
HiddenServiceEnableIntroDoSRatePerSec 25
HiddenServiceEnableIntroDoSBurstPerSec 200
## Proof-of-work defence for the service (needs a Tor build with PoW support; check the startup log).
HiddenServicePoWDefensesEnabled 1
HiddenServicePoWQueueRate 25
HiddenServicePoWQueueBurst 250
